1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,940
where victory lives on.

2
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:37,200
Plenty of girls are being kissed by plenty of boys they don't know and they do not care.

3
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:42,020
You can love it, hate it, embrace it, or turn away.

4
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:45,920
Lennon was shot to death late last night outside his apartment building.

5
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:48,980
But it is a past we all share.

6
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:50,640
Come on out here and give me a salute.

7
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:51,900
Big Davey salute.

8
00:00:51,901 --> 00:00:58,000
This is where yesterday has a home, where we wonder what it was like back then.

9
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:00,300
Go forward, knights, in safety.

10
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:02,000
And not too long ago.

11
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:05,000
His spirit must live on.

12
00:01:05,001 --> 00:01:09,840
It's where history has its place and where the past comes alive.

13
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:12,960
The History Channel.

14
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:42,000
In 1929, the Graf Zeppelin flew around the world with 20 passengers, almost four years

15
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:47,880
before the first solo flight around the globe by an airplane.

16
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:53,440
For 12 and a half days, the Graf's passengers dined on gourmet meals,

17
00:01:53,441 --> 00:02:02,080
listened to music and looked out the window as they passed leisurely over regions that no human

18
00:02:02,081 --> 00:02:09,760
being had ever seen from the air before this was airship travel at its peak an evolutionary

19
00:02:09,761 --> 00:02:13,960
voyage that began with a basic balloon and a lot of hot air

20
00:02:13,961 --> 00:02:24,160
man tried to fly for 5 000 years before he finally hit on the idea of the balloon

21
00:02:24,161 --> 00:02:32,280
with no place to go but up in 1783 the montgolfier brothers created the first balloon that rose under

22
00:02:32,281 --> 00:02:39,120
its own power hot air they were constantly going around with sponges and water to put out small

23
00:02:39,121 --> 00:02:42,687
fires. In December of that year, French chemist J.A.

24
00:02:42,688 --> 00:02:45,500
Charles made the first manned flight in a

25
00:02:45,501 --> 00:02:49,635
balloon filled with hydrogen, a much lighter source of lift.

26
00:02:49,636 --> 00:02:51,840
It was about seven times lighter

27
00:02:51,841 --> 00:02:58,300
than air, and it didn't take much to figure out that that would help support flight and

28
00:02:58,301 --> 00:03:03,420
especially manned flight. But hydrogen was considered flammable air because it burns

29
00:03:03,421 --> 00:03:06,920
furiously, especially when mixed with oxygen.

30
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:13,600
The problem now facing the early aeronauts was how to contain the lighter-than-air,

31
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:15,940
highly flammable hydrogen gas.

32
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:22,500
Fabric technology had progressed by that time, and they were starting to rubber coat balloons.

33
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:27,060
They found out, once you flew, let's go somewhere. We want to fly.

34
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:31,360
The balloon itself was a pretty limited vehicle. After all, you couldn't steer it.

35
00:03:31,361 --> 00:03:34,020
It simply floated to wherever the wind dug it.

36
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:38,100
A handful of aviators believe that the best hope for controlled flight

37
00:03:38,101 --> 00:03:41,540
was a motorized, steerable balloon, or dirigible.

38
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:48,480
Among them was a dashing young Brazilian balloonist named Alberto Santos Dumont,

39
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,100
who said trying to propel a dirigible balloon through the air

40
00:03:52,101 --> 00:03:55,420
is like pushing a candle through a brick wall.

41
00:03:56,420 --> 00:04:00,500
In 1884, two members of the French Army's Balloon Corps

42
00:04:00,501 --> 00:04:06,320
achieved an aeronautical first when they navigated their electric-powered balloon back to its

43
00:04:06,321 --> 00:04:09,336
original starting point against the wind.

44
00:04:09,337 --> 00:04:12,940
The 23-minute flight was cut short by the limited

45
00:04:12,941 --> 00:04:16,337
life of the engine's 500-pound batteries.

46
00:04:16,338 --> 00:04:19,900
Engine weight haunted the airship pioneers.

47
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:25,740
The challenge now was to create a balloon that was not only steerable, but streamlined and strong

48
00:04:25,741 --> 00:04:28,565
enough to lift men and machines.

49
00:04:28,566 --> 00:04:33,420
By 1897, Santos Dumont began work on his first airship.

50
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:41,180
Santos made a great leap forward in engine technology using a motorcycle-type motor

51
00:04:41,181 --> 00:04:47,920
for his airship and did some innovative work in propeller design that gave his ship greater

52
00:04:47,921 --> 00:04:48,621
efficiency.

53
00:04:48,622 --> 00:04:53,200
It was a smaller engine, giving him more lift, available lift for payload or for

54
00:04:53,201 --> 00:04:53,901
passengers.

55
00:04:53,902 --> 00:04:59,400
In order to maintain the internal gas pressure of the elongated and egg-shaped balloons,

56
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:06,620
aerial pioneers added ballonettes or airbags that expand and contract to compensate for changes in

57
00:05:06,621 --> 00:05:09,016
the pressure of the lifting gas.

58
00:05:09,017 --> 00:05:13,360
This basic principle is still used in dirigibles or blimps

59
00:05:13,361 --> 00:05:14,061
today.

60
00:05:14,062 --> 00:05:20,040
Soon the French added a rigid load-bearing keel to the elongated balloon to help distribute

61
00:05:20,041 --> 00:05:23,200
the enormous weight of the early electric and steam engines.

62
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:29,980
These semi-rigid airships were quite successful and used as military lookouts.

63
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:34,420
And then a man by the name of Count Zeppelin came along and said,

64
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:39,360
what would happen if we took a whole bunch of balloons and put them together and put a framework around them?

65
00:05:39,361 --> 00:05:42,460
And from that was born the Zeppelin airship.

66
00:05:42,461 --> 00:05:52,700
On July 2nd, 1900, a feisty Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin staked his fortune and reputation on a crazy idea.

67
00:05:53,260 --> 00:06:00,680
Three years before the Wright brothers' first flight, the Count's first rigid airship took flight over Lake Constance in southern Germany.

68
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:05,860
It flew for 18 minutes and a distance of three and a half miles.

69
00:06:05,861 --> 00:06:13,860
The LZ-1 for Luftschiff Zeppelin I was 416 feet long, 38 and a half feet in diameter,

70
00:06:13,861 --> 00:06:18,860
and had a lifting capacity of more than 27,000 pounds.

71
00:06:18,861 --> 00:06:19,860
How did he do it?

72
00:06:19,861 --> 00:06:23,860
Rather than fill one airbag with hydrogen as his predecessors had,

73
00:06:23,861 --> 00:06:33,860
the Count inflated 17 large gas cells and enclosed them in a rigid aerodynamic structure of aluminum alloy, or deralumin.

74
00:06:33,861 --> 00:06:40,100
The airship was powered by two engines with the combined horsepower of a Volkswagen Beetle.

75
00:06:40,101 --> 00:06:45,800
At the turn of the century, it was the invention of the internal combustion engine that helped

76
00:06:45,801 --> 00:06:48,957
make the Count's dream of a rigid airship a reality.

77
00:06:48,958 --> 00:06:50,960
But they were expensive to build.

78
00:06:50,961 --> 00:06:57,920
The German military said if Count Zeppelin can keep his airship in the air for 24 hours

79
00:06:57,921 --> 00:07:02,060
endurance, that they would be willing to purchase then his next airship.

80
00:07:02,061 --> 00:07:12,560
During one 24-hour endurance flight, Count von Zeppelin experienced engine trouble and put the airship down near the Daimler Engine Works and went to launch.

81
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:14,960
It would prove fortuitous.

82
00:07:16,220 --> 00:07:18,960
In the meantime, a storm came and blew the airship away.

83
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:27,160
Most unfortunate for the Count because it didn't fulfill his 24-hour endurance requirement for government sponsorship.

84
00:07:27,161 --> 00:07:33,160
But what happened was a spontaneous flood of donations came from the German people

85
00:07:33,161 --> 00:07:35,160
to help pay for the next airship.

86
00:07:35,161 --> 00:07:39,160
He received about 7.5 million gold marks from the loss of his ship

87
00:07:39,161 --> 00:07:46,160
and from all the donations that came in to build the Zeppelin company and to build future airships.

88
00:07:46,161 --> 00:07:48,160
It was a technical marvel.

89
00:07:48,161 --> 00:07:53,160
As the Zeppelin began to work the bugs out and began to be used more or less routinely,

90
00:07:53,161 --> 00:07:59,160
Just before World War I, there was a German Zeppelin commercial service called the DeLag.

91
00:07:59,161 --> 00:08:03,160
The different Zeppelins of the DeLag were making what we would call joyrides.

92
00:08:03,161 --> 00:08:14,160
You have to remember, aviation was totally new, and people really paid for their flights simply to have a bird's eye view of the city or town which they lived in.

93
00:08:14,161 --> 00:08:19,160
What's interesting to note, though, is a number of the passengers were not Germans.

94
00:08:19,161 --> 00:08:20,520
and not Germans.

95
00:08:20,521 --> 00:08:23,540
The enthusiasm of taking a flight on an airship

96
00:08:23,541 --> 00:08:26,700
had reached virtually every corner of the world.

97
00:08:27,700 --> 00:08:30,620
By 1914, the Daylog airships carried

98
00:08:30,621 --> 00:08:35,620
more than 34,000 passengers, over 100,000 miles.

99
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:39,900
Without a single accident, it was an economical success.

100
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:46,040
With the start of World War I,

101
00:08:46,041 --> 00:08:49,080
the Daylog commercial service came to an end,

102
00:08:49,081 --> 00:08:52,920
and Zeppelins were used for military purposes.

103
00:08:52,921 --> 00:08:57,480
The Zeppelin was touted as the great secret weapon

104
00:08:57,481 --> 00:09:00,540
that was going to revolutionize warfare.

105
00:09:00,541 --> 00:09:03,900
The German rigid airships reign supreme.

106
00:09:03,901 --> 00:09:06,700
Capable of flying above 11,000 feet,

107
00:09:06,701 --> 00:09:08,140
they were well out of the range

108
00:09:08,141 --> 00:09:10,600
of ground-based anti-aircraft guns,

109
00:09:10,601 --> 00:09:14,080
and not a single airship was lost to enemy fire.

110
00:09:14,081 --> 00:09:16,960
The German military ordered 26 airships

111
00:09:16,961 --> 00:09:18,660
during the first year of the war,

112
00:09:18,661 --> 00:09:21,920
and the Zeppelin company went into high gear.

113
00:09:21,921 --> 00:09:23,340
They were producing a rigid airship

114
00:09:23,341 --> 00:09:26,620
basically every two weeks off of one of the factories

115
00:09:26,621 --> 00:09:28,320
somewhere in Germany.

116
00:09:28,321 --> 00:09:32,480
It takes a million rivets to produce one airship.

117
00:09:32,481 --> 00:09:35,360
It also takes 700,000 skins,

118
00:09:35,361 --> 00:09:38,580
that is the internal intestines of a cow,

119
00:09:38,581 --> 00:09:41,480
to make all the gas bags for one airship.

120
00:09:41,481 --> 00:09:43,560
It was a huge wartime effort.

121
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:46,700
Zeppelins were used by the German military

122
00:09:46,701 --> 00:09:51,340
for reconnaissance, as well as bombing raids over Great Britain.

123
00:09:51,341 --> 00:09:54,300
They were above the clouds oftentimes.

124
00:09:54,301 --> 00:09:57,440
They did try using radio as a means of navigation.

125
00:09:57,441 --> 00:10:00,940
And many a raid took place by Zeppelin,

126
00:10:00,941 --> 00:10:03,820
in which the commander would report that he raided this place

127
00:10:03,821 --> 00:10:05,760
or that place, and he was nowhere near it.

128
00:10:05,761 --> 00:10:07,560
There was no bombing accuracy.

129
00:10:07,561 --> 00:10:10,220
On the other hand, the airships did a large amount

130
00:10:10,221 --> 00:10:13,660
of reconnaissance work, basically flying over the sea,

131
00:10:13,661 --> 00:10:15,160
watching out for the British Navy.

132
00:10:16,700 --> 00:10:24,860
As the war escalated, the Germans built lighter and lighter airships, capable of operating at an altitude of 20,000 feet.

133
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:36,879
Frustrated by their lack of rigid airship technology, the British concentrated on building fighter planes, capable of climbing to 22,000 feet, and ammunition that

134
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:39,660
could bring the high-flying Zeppelins down.

135
00:10:39,661 --> 00:10:44,660
The thing that really did the airships in during the First World War was the development

136
00:10:44,661 --> 00:10:47,160
by the British of the so-called incendiary bullet.

137
00:10:47,161 --> 00:10:51,940
A bullet like this hitting an airship filled with hydrogen meant one bullet was all it

138
00:10:51,941 --> 00:10:54,340
took to bring an airship down in flames.

139
00:10:54,341 --> 00:11:01,620
It was not the great Zeppelin airships that revolutionized warfare in World War I.

140
00:11:01,621 --> 00:11:08,580
Instead it turned out to be a small incendiary bullet.

141
00:11:08,581 --> 00:11:14,940
In March of 1917, at the age of 78, Count von Zeppelin died.

142
00:11:14,941 --> 00:11:25,280
Two Zeppelins dropped flowers as his coffin was lowered into the earth.

143
00:11:25,281 --> 00:11:30,040
At the end of World War I, the great German Zeppelin company was forbidden from building

144
00:11:30,041 --> 00:11:33,680
any more airships by the Treaty of Versailles.

145
00:11:33,681 --> 00:11:38,160
The remaining Zeppelin airships were to be divided up among the Allies.

146
00:11:38,161 --> 00:11:44,520
But the German airship crews basically destroyed the majority of the airships in their hangars

147
00:11:44,521 --> 00:11:47,400
before the treaty went into effect.

148
00:11:47,401 --> 00:11:52,180
The airships that the Germans did give over, for example, to the French, they didn't give

149
00:11:52,181 --> 00:11:56,640
them the technology as to how to operate them and how to maintain them.

150
00:11:56,641 --> 00:11:59,660
That they kept unto themselves.

151
00:11:59,661 --> 00:12:04,680
By 1921, the United States was interested in getting its own airship.

152
00:12:04,681 --> 00:12:08,860
They ordered a British-built version of the high-flying German airships, and during the

153
00:12:08,861 --> 00:12:15,180
final stress test at sea, the R-38 snapped in two, killing 44 sailors.

154
00:12:15,181 --> 00:12:19,860
The airship's light frame buckled under the pressure of high-speed turns.

155
00:12:19,861 --> 00:12:24,820
Despite considerable apprehension from the Allies, the United States ordered an airship

156
00:12:24,821 --> 00:12:29,060
from the cash-strapped Zeppelin Company as part of war reparations.

157
00:12:29,061 --> 00:12:32,960
The ship was to be delivered in 1922.

158
00:12:32,961 --> 00:12:37,000
In the meantime, the Americans decided to build their own airship.

159
00:12:37,001 --> 00:12:39,300
They would call it the Shenandoah.

160
00:12:39,301 --> 00:12:44,020
During the war, a German Zeppelin came down in France and the Allies had a chance to inspect

161
00:12:44,021 --> 00:12:45,020
it.

162
00:12:45,021 --> 00:12:46,820
This was the L-49.

163
00:12:46,821 --> 00:12:49,800
And as a result, they generated some drawings.

164
00:12:49,801 --> 00:12:55,580
The Navy acquired these drawings and basically built their own Zeppelin in Philadelphia.

165
00:12:55,581 --> 00:13:00,660
The technology was very much World War I technology on the Shenandoah.

166
00:13:00,661 --> 00:13:02,907
were some American refinements and improvements.

167
00:13:02,908 --> 00:13:05,200
And of course, it flew with helium, not hydrogen.

168
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:10,880
Helium is an inert gas first discovered in a few Texas oil wells.

169
00:13:10,881 --> 00:13:13,040
In 1922, the gas was in short

170
00:13:13,041 --> 00:13:18,920
supply and very expensive. And even though helium provided less lift than hydrogen,

171
00:13:18,921 --> 00:13:22,523
its primary advantage was that it does not burn.

172
00:13:22,524 --> 00:13:25,300
The golden age of airships had begun.

173
00:13:25,301 --> 00:13:32,300
The Shenandoah's first flight from Lakehurst, New Jersey marked the beginning of the era of American rigid airships.

174
00:13:32,301 --> 00:13:36,300
Americans were enchanted by the ship's flights around the country.

175
00:13:36,301 --> 00:13:40,300
She was hailed as the forerunner to a commercial passenger airship.

176
00:13:40,301 --> 00:13:43,300
However, her service was cut short.

177
00:13:43,301 --> 00:13:49,300
In 1925, the Shenandoah got caught in a severe Midwest thunderstorm and broke in half.

178
00:13:49,301 --> 00:13:55,440
The 210 foot long forward section carrying seven men shot up to 10,000 feet.

179
00:13:55,980 --> 00:14:02,300
Lieutenant Commander Charles Rosendahl figured he could fly what was left of the airship under the same principles as a balloon.

180
00:14:02,940 --> 00:14:11,600
By dumping gasoline and valving off helium, he was able to fly what was left of the ship for almost two hours before the forward section of the Shenandoah crashed.

181
00:14:12,420 --> 00:14:16,000
While badly injured, Rosendahl and his six men survived.

182
00:14:16,580 --> 00:14:18,260
14 others died.

183
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:33,300
In October of 1924, Americans were able to get their first look at their second rigid airship, the German-built LZ-126.

184
00:14:35,660 --> 00:14:44,141
This was the state-of-the-art airship design for the 1920s, and the Germans believed it would be the last up when they would ever build, so they put extra care into

185
00:14:44,142 --> 00:14:45,020
its construction.

186
00:14:45,021 --> 00:14:47,335
The LZ-126 was the vision of Dr.

187
00:14:47,336 --> 00:14:55,800
Hugo Eckner, a business savvy and cautious Zeppelin pilot who took over the company after Count von Zeppelin's death.

188
00:14:56,320 --> 00:15:02,900
His favorite task was using the Zeppelin as a means of increasing friendship between nations.

189
00:15:03,100 --> 00:15:09,720
To him, the Zeppelin provided this means of international trade and commerce.

190
00:15:09,721 --> 00:15:12,720
Echner was a very unique businessman,

191
00:15:12,721 --> 00:15:16,720
and that is very evident through the LZ-126,

192
00:15:16,721 --> 00:15:18,720
because when the ship was completed

193
00:15:18,721 --> 00:15:21,720
and due to make its flight to the United States,

194
00:15:21,721 --> 00:15:23,720
no insurance company was willing to take the risk.

195
00:15:23,721 --> 00:15:26,720
But he was so sure of the technology of that airship

196
00:15:26,721 --> 00:15:29,720
and that the flight would be successful,

197
00:15:29,721 --> 00:15:32,720
that he said, okay, we will insure the flight ourselves.

198
00:15:32,721 --> 00:15:35,720
But he knew that the whole future of the Zeppelin cause

199
00:15:35,721 --> 00:15:38,720
was at stake. He had to deliver that airship.

200
00:15:38,721 --> 00:15:41,122
airship. He took the risk and it paid off.

201
00:15:41,123 --> 00:15:44,440
But he did land with very few gallons of fuel in his tank.

202
00:15:44,780 --> 00:15:49,960
Upon his arrival in the States, Eckner took a leisurely flight over Manhattan before delivering

203
00:15:49,961 --> 00:15:53,569
the ship to the Navy base in Lakehurst, New Jersey.

204
00:15:53,570 --> 00:15:56,400
The Navy immediately replaced the ship's

205
00:15:56,401 --> 00:16:00,460
hydrogen with non-flammable helium and renamed her Los Angeles.

206
00:16:02,480 --> 00:16:05,226
You know, I see this thing in front of the hangar coming in.

207
00:16:05,227 --> 00:16:06,920
I didn't know what the heck I saw. It

208
00:16:06,921 --> 00:16:10,880
were so big, I mean, and it looked like a mountain

209
00:16:10,881 --> 00:16:12,660
was floating in there.

210
00:16:12,661 --> 00:16:15,780
It was given to the Navy to use for experimental

211
00:16:15,781 --> 00:16:17,700
and training purposes.

212
00:16:17,701 --> 00:16:20,860
And it wasn't supposed to be used for war purposes

213
00:16:20,861 --> 00:16:22,000
or anything like that.

214
00:16:22,001 --> 00:16:24,000
The Los Angeles went on to have a long

215
00:16:24,001 --> 00:16:27,400
and distinguished career as a Navy training ship.

216
00:16:27,401 --> 00:16:30,160
She flew more than 4,000 hours,

217
00:16:30,161 --> 00:16:32,600
the most of any American rigid airship.

218
00:16:32,601 --> 00:16:39,140
While the Germans were trying to re-establish a tradition of building rigid airships, the

219
00:16:39,141 --> 00:16:42,380
British were having trouble with their designs.

220
00:16:42,381 --> 00:16:47,520
Using downed zeppelins from World War I as models, their technology was dated.

221
00:16:47,521 --> 00:16:52,500
Nevertheless, in 1930, the British rolled out one of the most unique airships to date.

222
00:16:52,501 --> 00:16:56,760
The R101 featured promenades and panoramic views.

223
00:16:56,761 --> 00:17:02,200
At the last minute, an additional airbag was needed in order to lift the lavish ship.

224
00:17:02,201 --> 00:17:08,600
The British seemed to have a flair for putting on too many things that were not necessary.

225
00:17:09,320 --> 00:17:13,540
A ship's bell, for example, that probably weighed, you know, 20, 40 pounds.

226
00:17:14,120 --> 00:17:16,320
Things like that added up very quickly.

227
00:17:16,760 --> 00:17:21,440
Fifty-four passengers boarded the R-101 for her maiden voyage to India.

228
00:17:22,320 --> 00:17:26,320
150 miles from England, the airship crashed in the French countryside,

229
00:17:26,321 --> 00:17:30,680
killing 48 people, as well as the British rigid airship program.

230
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:37,200
The American airship program, on the other hand, was about to change.

231
00:17:37,201 --> 00:17:40,200
Rich in helium, yet lacking the necessary technology,

232
00:17:40,201 --> 00:17:44,200
the Americans went into business with the airship experienced Germans

233
00:17:44,201 --> 00:17:47,200
forming the Goodyear Zeppelin Company.

234
00:17:47,201 --> 00:17:50,200
Here was an American firm who believed in building zeppelins.

235
00:17:50,201 --> 00:17:53,200
P.W. Litchfield, who was the president of Goodyear,

236
00:17:53,201 --> 00:17:59,200
was very much an airship advocate, and he negotiated to acquire the zeppelin patents.

237
00:17:59,201 --> 00:18:00,200
Zeppelin patents.

238
00:18:00,201 --> 00:18:02,400
They not only acquired the patent rights,

239
00:18:02,401 --> 00:18:06,780
but they acquired 13 of the Zeppelin company's

240
00:18:06,781 --> 00:18:10,940
leading technologists, including Dr. Carl Arnstein,

241
00:18:10,941 --> 00:18:12,920
who had been one of the chief engineers

242
00:18:12,921 --> 00:18:15,120
in the Zeppelin company.

243
00:18:15,121 --> 00:18:16,720
He brought these other 12 over.

244
00:18:16,721 --> 00:18:20,220
They were known, incidentally, as the 12 disciples.

245
00:18:20,221 --> 00:18:23,220
They began work immediately on the USS Akron,

246
00:18:23,221 --> 00:18:27,100
the most innovative airship of her time.

247
00:18:27,101 --> 00:18:30,240
Construction started in the autumn of 1929,

248
00:18:30,241 --> 00:18:33,680
and it was finished by the summer of 1931.

249
00:18:33,681 --> 00:18:36,160
She was a battleship of the sky.

250
00:18:36,161 --> 00:18:38,160
The USS Akron was the first ship,

251
00:18:38,161 --> 00:18:41,500
and that was different in that it was designed from the outset

252
00:18:41,501 --> 00:18:43,660
to be a flying aircraft carrier.

253
00:18:43,661 --> 00:18:46,380
Inside the belly of the airship, there was a hangar bay,

254
00:18:46,381 --> 00:18:48,720
and it could carry up to five biplanes.

255
00:18:48,721 --> 00:18:51,880
They had several planes, but the most famous was the Sparrowhawk,

256
00:18:51,881 --> 00:18:56,480
a little bit of a 20-foot wingspan fighter.

257
00:18:56,481 --> 00:18:58,480
It would come up, had a hook on the top,

258
00:18:58,481 --> 00:19:01,460
and it would hook onto a trapeze bar

259
00:19:01,461 --> 00:19:03,820
from a trapeze that lowered from the ship.

260
00:19:03,821 --> 00:19:07,320
It would engage, and then the trapeze would raise the ship up

261
00:19:07,321 --> 00:19:08,660
and stow it in a hangar.

262
00:19:11,060 --> 00:19:13,820
And the idea behind this was that the planes could fly out

263
00:19:13,821 --> 00:19:15,940
and scout at a much greater distance

264
00:19:15,941 --> 00:19:17,660
than the airship alone.

265
00:19:17,661 --> 00:19:20,480
The Ekron had three reinforced keels

266
00:19:20,481 --> 00:19:23,100
and eight engines capable of driving the ship

267
00:19:23,101 --> 00:19:25,200
at nearly 70 miles per hour.

268
00:19:25,201 --> 00:19:31,260
The engines were inside the ship and a log shaft came out there on the propellers and

269
00:19:31,261 --> 00:19:36,240
you just walk through a door into the engine compartment.

270
00:19:36,241 --> 00:19:43,600
In 1933, after logging more than 1,700 flight hours, the 200-ton airship flew into a storm

271
00:19:43,601 --> 00:19:48,280
off the New Jersey coast and hit the water at full speed.

272
00:19:48,281 --> 00:19:51,200
73 of the 76 men perished.

273
00:19:51,201 --> 00:19:54,980
There were no life jackets on board.

274
00:19:54,981 --> 00:19:57,280
Nearly two weeks after the crash of the Akron,

275
00:19:57,281 --> 00:19:59,300
her sister ship was completed.

276
00:19:59,301 --> 00:20:02,180
The USS Macon was identical except that she was

277
00:20:02,181 --> 00:20:05,420
8,000 pounds lighter and faster.

278
00:20:05,421 --> 00:20:07,020
The fact the new commanding officer

279
00:20:07,021 --> 00:20:08,720
found difficult to deal with.

280
00:20:09,920 --> 00:20:12,820
As one senior airship officer remarked to me,

281
00:20:12,821 --> 00:20:16,440
he was so careful it was downright embarrassing.

282
00:20:16,441 --> 00:20:18,560
And you can't, you can't really blame him.

283
00:20:18,561 --> 00:20:20,820
I mean, he takes command of a ship.

284
00:20:20,821 --> 00:20:23,740
The preceding ship was destroyed only two weeks before.

285
00:20:24,980 --> 00:20:28,605
10,000 miles without refueling. She's the pride of the Navy.

286
00:20:28,606 --> 00:20:30,840
Using its fighter planes as lookouts,

287
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:37,822
the Macon could patrol almost 129,000 square miles of ocean in a day.

288
00:20:37,823 --> 00:20:39,800
In February of 1935,

289
00:20:40,100 --> 00:20:44,840
the Macon was lost in severe weather off the coast of California near Point Sur.

290
00:20:46,740 --> 00:20:52,640
This time, they had life jackets on board and all but two of the 83 men on board survived.

291
00:20:52,641 --> 00:20:57,640
When the Macon sank, the Navy was not interested enough

292
00:20:57,740 --> 00:21:00,040
in wanting to recover or do anything

293
00:21:00,140 --> 00:21:01,640
about bringing any of it back up.

294
00:21:01,740 --> 00:21:04,720
They figured they'd spent enough money down the drain

295
00:21:04,820 --> 00:21:08,160
and had gotten nothing really from it except wrecks.

296
00:21:12,220 --> 00:21:17,460
On July 8th, 1928, the LZ-127, or Graf Zeppelin,

297
00:21:17,560 --> 00:21:20,560
was christened by Count von Zeppelin's daughter.

298
00:21:20,561 --> 00:21:26,060
Nearly 10 stories high, the ship cleared the hangar door by only two feet.

299
00:21:26,061 --> 00:21:29,800
With the easing of sanctions imposed by the Versailles Treaty,

300
00:21:29,801 --> 00:21:32,600
the Germans were back in the Zeppelin business for themselves.

301
00:21:32,601 --> 00:21:37,600
At the helm was Dr. Hugo Eckner, the Zeppelin's greatest champion.

302
00:21:37,601 --> 00:21:40,560
This was the period of empire building.

303
00:21:40,561 --> 00:21:46,560
The vision of Dr. Eckner was to see an intercontinental airship passenger service.

304
00:21:46,561 --> 00:21:53,660
At 775 feet, the Graf Zeppelin was two and a half football fields long,

305
00:21:53,661 --> 00:21:58,740
and in early October, she set out on the first trans-oceanic voyage

306
00:21:58,741 --> 00:22:00,840
by an airship with passengers.

307
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:07,440
It was designed to prove the airworthiness of rigid airships.

308
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:11,020
That was Dr. Eckner's primary concern,

309
00:22:11,140 --> 00:22:14,540
because you have to remember, at this time, the early airlines,

310
00:22:14,541 --> 00:22:17,080
they were starting to make a name for themselves.

311
00:22:17,620 --> 00:22:21,720
And so airships, due to the dangerous nature of hydrogen,

312
00:22:22,720 --> 00:22:24,000
due to their size,

313
00:22:24,540 --> 00:22:28,200
tended to be still considered as a frail means of transportation.

314
00:22:28,960 --> 00:22:32,160
And so the 127 was really a very experimental airship.

315
00:22:33,140 --> 00:22:35,600
Equipped with two sets of gas cells,

316
00:22:35,760 --> 00:22:38,640
one for engine fuel and another for lifting gas,

317
00:22:39,240 --> 00:22:42,200
the Graf Zeppelin arrived safely in the United States.

318
00:22:42,201 --> 00:22:47,880
The airship was met with great fanfare as thousands of people flocked to get a glimpse of her.

319
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:54,100
In bold pursuit of his dream of regular commercial transcontinental airship service,

320
00:22:54,520 --> 00:22:57,920
Hugo Eckner vowed to fly the Graf Zeppelin around the world,

321
00:22:58,480 --> 00:23:01,140
not in a fictional 80 days, but in two weeks.

322
00:23:01,740 --> 00:23:04,940
The trip was projected to cost more than $250,000,

323
00:23:05,560 --> 00:23:07,960
money the Zeppelin company didn't have.

324
00:23:09,080 --> 00:23:11,240
As a friend of his once remarked to me,

325
00:23:11,241 --> 00:23:17,240
you know it's remarkable that he kept that company going at the depths of the depression, particularly in Germany.

326
00:23:17,560 --> 00:23:20,640
So he knew how to get money and make money and the like.

327
00:23:20,880 --> 00:23:24,280
So too did newspaper magnate William Randolph Hearst,

328
00:23:24,680 --> 00:23:29,980
who immediately offered $150,000 to Eckner for the exclusive rights to cover the trip.

329
00:23:30,740 --> 00:23:33,540
Incredibly, the rest of the money came from stamp collectors.

330
00:23:34,360 --> 00:23:39,140
Notices would appear even in the New York Times or the papers in this country and everywhere else

331
00:23:39,141 --> 00:23:41,600
that the ship was going to make this record flight,

332
00:23:41,740 --> 00:23:46,140
and now is your chance, everyone, to get some mail with a special stamp on it

333
00:23:46,141 --> 00:23:48,120
carried on the Graf Zeppelin.

334
00:23:51,780 --> 00:23:56,700
The flight of the Graf Zeppelin provided classic newsreel images seen worldwide.

335
00:23:56,701 --> 00:24:02,800
To the magic of motion pictures, you will share with us the thrill and enjoyment

336
00:24:02,801 --> 00:24:06,480
of perhaps the greatest aerial venture in all history.

337
00:24:09,140 --> 00:24:12,220
We are now well out over the Atlantic.

338
00:24:13,840 --> 00:24:16,240
We are making 80 miles per hour.

339
00:24:17,960 --> 00:24:20,360
Inspecting the outer covering of the Zeppelin,

340
00:24:21,280 --> 00:24:24,980
this chap makes certain to hold on to that rope.

341
00:24:26,580 --> 00:24:29,140
Several correspondents were on board,

342
00:24:29,440 --> 00:24:32,260
busy writing their stories for the Hearst newspapers.

343
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:38,080
This is the dining room.

344
00:24:38,081 --> 00:24:45,080
comfortable, even luxurious, worthy of any first-class hotel on land.

345
00:24:47,080 --> 00:24:51,080
And now as we sail, let's listen a while.

346
00:24:51,081 --> 00:25:04,580
England at last.

347
00:25:04,581 --> 00:25:10,360
The Graf crossed the Atlantic in a record 42 hours and continued on her way to Germany.

348
00:25:11,420 --> 00:25:18,220
Berlin, where thousands in the streets below rejoice over the Zeppelin's triumph.

349
00:25:21,080 --> 00:25:25,740
The land of the rising sun in sight.

350
00:25:37,920 --> 00:25:41,840
And now for the unexplored skies of the Pacific.

351
00:25:42,680 --> 00:25:46,400
On the first nonstop flight ever made.

352
00:25:46,401 --> 00:25:55,420
Most of the time we fly in a strange uncanny universe with limitless seas of close-packed clouds

353
00:25:55,421 --> 00:26:03,600
Below a world in which time means little and the ordinary perceptions of distance even less

354
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:21,400
Our first glimpse of American territory.

355
00:26:26,400 --> 00:26:28,400
Directly over the Golden Gate,

356
00:26:28,401 --> 00:26:33,400
the sleek silver airship approaches San Francisco.

357
00:26:33,401 --> 00:26:38,400
The graph flies very, very low over the heart of the city.

358
00:26:38,401 --> 00:26:41,400
Occasionally, we are shaken up a bit,

359
00:26:41,401 --> 00:26:45,400
but we float safely over the mountains of the great southwest.

360
00:26:45,401 --> 00:26:51,600
What an inspiring view of the grandeur that is America we are getting.

361
00:26:52,560 --> 00:26:59,340
A voyage such as we have made is truly not only an education in geography, but a lesson in patriotism.

362
00:27:02,540 --> 00:27:07,240
Chicago is giving us as great a thrill as the Zeppelin is giving Chicago.

363
00:27:07,241 --> 00:27:17,820
In 12 flying days, we have traveled some 20,000 miles, spanned two continents, looked down

364
00:27:17,821 --> 00:27:25,960
upon ten nations, conquered mountains and sea, and here we are, journey's end in sight.

365
00:27:30,040 --> 00:27:33,640
After successfully completing a trip around the world, Dr.

366
00:27:33,641 --> 00:27:35,440
Eckner was hailed in New York

367
00:27:35,441 --> 00:27:38,840
as a hero and considered one of the most famous men in the world.

368
00:27:38,841 --> 00:27:45,900
This flight has convinced me that the airship has passed the experimental stage.

369
00:27:46,840 --> 00:27:47,840
Indeed it had.

370
00:27:48,280 --> 00:27:52,080
The 7,000-mile nonstop flight from Germany to Japan

371
00:27:52,081 --> 00:27:55,160
was proof positive for many airship skeptics.

372
00:27:56,100 --> 00:27:59,820
She still had enough fuel on board to make another two days in the air.

373
00:28:00,340 --> 00:28:04,540
So that means the rigid airship could have almost stayed up in the air,

374
00:28:04,541 --> 00:28:09,600
non-stop for a week carrying passengers and safely.

375
00:28:11,660 --> 00:28:15,320
Soon, Eckner pioneered passenger service to South America.

376
00:28:16,080 --> 00:28:19,740
Only the well-to-do could afford a flight on an airship.

377
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:22,900
The early flights, or these flights to North and South America,

378
00:28:23,160 --> 00:28:25,060
cost approximately 1,000 Reismark,

379
00:28:25,680 --> 00:28:31,820
which was several years' income for a normal German worker at the time.

380
00:28:31,821 --> 00:28:38,120
It only took two and a half days to North America and three and a half days to South America, nonstop.

381
00:28:38,680 --> 00:28:44,320
Even the airplanes from Pan Am were not able to make this type of a trip.

382
00:28:44,740 --> 00:28:46,640
It was the fastest means of getting there.

383
00:28:47,800 --> 00:28:55,640
With still more skies to fly, Eckner set off on a scientific expedition to the North Pole in July of 1931.

384
00:28:56,140 --> 00:28:59,800
Not only did they bring back information about the Arctic's atmosphere,

385
00:28:59,801 --> 00:29:03,740
but the Graf Zeppelin proved she could fly in extreme conditions.

386
00:29:04,360 --> 00:29:08,860
Here again, cold weather didn't affect the operation of the airship at all.

387
00:29:09,300 --> 00:29:13,840
And so that was the big success and the big reasoning behind the 127.

388
00:29:15,080 --> 00:29:18,800
After that, Eckner could prove and safely say,

389
00:29:18,920 --> 00:29:23,620
we can start an intercontinental passenger airship service and make it work.

390
00:29:24,460 --> 00:29:26,600
While the Graf Zeppelin ruled the skies,

391
00:29:26,601 --> 00:29:30,480
Hitler and the Nazi Party were navigating to rule Germany.

392
00:29:31,140 --> 00:29:33,420
If Eckner was to continue building his dream,

393
00:29:33,860 --> 00:29:36,660
he would soon have to make a deal with the devil.

394
00:29:40,240 --> 00:29:44,420
By 1933, Hitler and the Nazi Party controlled Germany.

395
00:29:44,940 --> 00:29:47,800
They also controlled the fate of Dr. Eckner

396
00:29:47,801 --> 00:29:50,940
and his dream of intercontinental airship service.

397
00:29:50,941 --> 00:30:02,240
I hope I will be able in about six or seven weeks to take over the new airship to the United States of America.

398
00:30:03,060 --> 00:30:12,380
He was asked at one time to let Hitler speak at a political rally in the German Zeppelin company hangars at Friedrichshafen, and he refused.

399
00:30:13,360 --> 00:30:17,180
Well, you didn't do that and still remain very friendly with the Nazis.

400
00:30:17,181 --> 00:30:22,720
Echner was an outspoken critic of the Nazi party and was only spared from the

401
00:30:22,721 --> 00:30:26,460
concentration camps because of his popularity throughout the world.

402
00:30:26,461 --> 00:30:31,220
Because of the fame that he'd acquired in the trips around the world and the like,

403
00:30:31,221 --> 00:30:38,060
the Nazis wanted to make sure they could use Echner for promotional and publicity and propaganda purposes.

404
00:30:38,061 --> 00:30:42,640
Dr. Echner did not like the Nazis. That is very obvious.

405
00:30:42,641 --> 00:30:47,060
But on the other hand, he saw the necessity to play with the devil, if you will.

406
00:30:47,240 --> 00:30:48,435
And that gave Dr.

407
00:30:48,436 --> 00:30:53,920
Eckner the funds that he needed to begin his intercontinental airship service.

408
00:30:54,980 --> 00:30:56,640
The deal was this.

409
00:30:57,140 --> 00:31:03,920
Eckner needed money to build bigger hangars that could house an entire fleet of airships large enough to service the major cities of the world.

410
00:31:04,800 --> 00:31:10,800
Hitler agreed to finance Eckner's plans and formed an operating company which would control the airships out of Frankfurt.

411
00:31:12,640 --> 00:31:18,580
Early in 1936, one of Eckner's dream ships became a reality with the unveiling of the Hindenburg,

412
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:23,280
the epitome of everything the Germans had learned about rigid airships.

413
00:31:23,680 --> 00:31:26,320
She was the largest airship the world would ever see.

414
00:31:27,100 --> 00:31:29,860
I saw the Hindenburg, and I've never forgotten it.

415
00:31:30,520 --> 00:31:33,880
And I don't think anybody else ever did who did see it.

416
00:31:34,880 --> 00:31:42,280
At a length of 804 feet, she was 572 feet longer than a 747 jumbo jet.

417
00:31:42,640 --> 00:31:45,480
and only 78 feet shorter than the Titanic.

418
00:31:46,260 --> 00:31:50,880
She weighed 130 tons and was as high as a 13-story building.

419
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:56,880
The Hindenburg had a gas capacity of more than 7 million cubic feet,

420
00:31:56,881 --> 00:32:00,160
the volume of 300 average American homes.

421
00:32:01,100 --> 00:32:07,420
She was powered by four Daimler-Benz diesel engines capable of 1,320 horsepower each.

422
00:32:07,421 --> 00:32:13,580
where aluminum alloy girders laid end-to-end would stretch for 13 miles.

423
00:32:14,740 --> 00:32:20,700
To Eckner's dismay, however, huge swastikas emblazoned the ship's 105-foot tail fins.

424
00:32:21,180 --> 00:32:25,480
And it wasn't long before the Hindenburg and the beloved Graf Zeppelin

425
00:32:25,481 --> 00:32:27,820
went to work for the Nazi party.

426
00:32:28,920 --> 00:32:33,460
The Nazis needed the ash ships for propaganda purposes with the big swastikas on the tail,

427
00:32:33,940 --> 00:32:36,480
and Eckner had a fit about that.

428
00:32:36,481 --> 00:32:46,820
Shortly after the Hindenburg was completed, the airships traveled to German cities of 100,000 or more, dropping leaflets promoting Hitler and Nazi Party platforms.

429
00:32:47,460 --> 00:32:56,760
They continued to exploit the massive airship for political purposes at the Nazi Party's Congress in Nuremberg and the 1936 Olympic Games in Berlin.

430
00:32:58,120 --> 00:33:05,580
The Nazis replaced Eckner as the ship's cautious and conservative captain in favor of a pilot that was more politically acceptable.

431
00:33:05,581 --> 00:33:08,340
a move that didn't sit well with Eckner

432
00:33:08,341 --> 00:33:11,400
when the new pilot damaged the Hindenburg's tail fin.

433
00:33:12,900 --> 00:33:15,060
Eckner storms up to Lehmann,

434
00:33:15,280 --> 00:33:18,700
who was the captain in command of it at that time,

435
00:33:19,160 --> 00:33:20,780
and in a loud voice said,

436
00:33:20,880 --> 00:33:23,280
how could you take our brand new airship out

437
00:33:23,281 --> 00:33:27,400
just to please your lord and master Adolf Hitler?

438
00:33:28,080 --> 00:33:30,940
As a result, the press people were informed

439
00:33:30,941 --> 00:33:33,760
that from henceforth, Eckner's name and picture

440
00:33:33,761 --> 00:33:37,080
were not ever to be mentioned in the Nazi press again.

441
00:33:37,920 --> 00:33:41,980
So he was quite a brave fellow in that respect.

442
00:33:45,240 --> 00:33:50,060
When Hindenburg made its first trip to this country in 1936,

443
00:33:50,640 --> 00:33:53,580
Eckner was on board, but he had no title and no authority.

444
00:33:53,740 --> 00:33:55,400
They went around him, essentially.

445
00:33:56,020 --> 00:33:59,480
Of all the man-made birds that fly, this was the greatest.

446
00:33:59,481 --> 00:34:06,480
The Hindenburg was built above all for intercontinental flight, and eventually that's what she did.

447
00:34:06,481 --> 00:34:13,480
In 1936, the airship made seven scheduled round trips to South America and ten to North America.

448
00:34:13,481 --> 00:34:18,480
The Hindenburg was the largest man-made thing that ever flew.

449
00:34:18,481 --> 00:34:23,480
And it normally flew at altitudes of a thousand feet or less.

450
00:34:23,481 --> 00:34:28,660
Now, when you had something three-city blockless long flying directly overhead,

451
00:34:29,200 --> 00:34:32,240
making very little noise, it left a tremendous impression.

452
00:34:33,120 --> 00:34:40,160
Just as Eckner had imagined, the Hindenburgs' passenger accommodations set a new standard in aircraft luxury.

453
00:34:41,580 --> 00:34:44,100
They had every comfort dreamable on board.

454
00:34:45,020 --> 00:34:48,080
Gourmet meals were cooked in an electric kitchen.

455
00:34:48,960 --> 00:34:52,600
The passengers had two bunks. There was two beds in a room.

456
00:34:52,601 --> 00:34:55,600
Each room was equipped with hot and cold running water.

457
00:34:55,980 --> 00:34:56,920
There was also a shower.

458
00:34:57,200 --> 00:35:00,140
There was a writing room, which also had a piano on board.

459
00:35:00,460 --> 00:35:04,560
Of course, the piano was out of aluminum and didn't weigh more than about 40 pounds.

460
00:35:05,280 --> 00:35:08,660
The Hindenburg even had its own special smoking room,

461
00:35:08,820 --> 00:35:13,480
which was pressurized to prevent any hydrogen gas from entering the room.

462
00:35:14,340 --> 00:35:19,380
In the smoking room was one lighter, which was shared among all smokers,

463
00:35:19,381 --> 00:35:21,540
and you could only smoke with inside that room,

464
00:35:21,660 --> 00:35:24,160
which made it, of course, a very unique airship.

465
00:35:25,060 --> 00:35:28,460
Passengers paid $400 one way to North America

466
00:35:28,461 --> 00:35:30,740
and 720 round trip.

467
00:35:32,240 --> 00:35:34,380
Filled with highly flammable hydrogen,

468
00:35:34,540 --> 00:35:36,140
the crew wore felt booties

469
00:35:36,141 --> 00:35:38,900
to prevent even the smallest spark from friction.

470
00:35:39,960 --> 00:35:42,320
But attention to the smallest detail

471
00:35:42,321 --> 00:35:45,040
would not be enough to prevent Hugo Eckner's dream

472
00:35:45,041 --> 00:35:46,620
from going up in smoke.

473
00:35:49,380 --> 00:36:00,360
Only a handful of reporters were present in 1937 when the Hindenburg made her final approach to the Navy base in Lakehurst, New Jersey.

474
00:36:01,120 --> 00:36:06,100
After all, Eckner's dream ship had made 17 transcontinental flights the year before.

475
00:36:06,800 --> 00:36:10,340
John Ionicone was working that day on the Navy ground crew.

476
00:36:10,880 --> 00:36:17,020
He was one of 198 sailors on station and the only military man still alive who saw what happened.

477
00:36:17,021 --> 00:36:21,780
They were supposed to come in at 6 o'clock that morning, but it was 12 hours late.

478
00:36:21,781 --> 00:36:26,900
It had been raining and thunder and lightning and a lot of clouds

479
00:36:26,901 --> 00:36:28,280
crammed off to the base.

480
00:36:28,281 --> 00:36:31,520
We wouldn't let them land, and so they just flew around.

481
00:36:31,521 --> 00:36:35,780
And then around 6 o'clock, they told them to come back, because it had started to clear up.

482
00:36:35,781 --> 00:36:37,780
In the meantime, the ship got all wet.

483
00:36:37,781 --> 00:36:42,800
They were coming in for a landing, and we could see the whole ship from where we were

484
00:36:42,801 --> 00:36:43,800
all the time.

485
00:36:43,801 --> 00:36:48,960
dropped the lines and the ground crew had a hold of the two nose lines.

486
00:36:48,961 --> 00:36:51,000
It was a routine land. Everything was routine.

487
00:36:51,500 --> 00:36:56,400
And then all of a sudden right there in front of the tail I saw a big red glow inside the ship.

488
00:36:57,180 --> 00:37:00,140
And then it broke through the cover and it just burned.

489
00:37:00,260 --> 00:37:02,640
And it's crashing. It's crashing, terrible.

490
00:37:03,080 --> 00:37:04,700
Oh my, get out of the way, please.

491
00:37:05,020 --> 00:37:08,220
It's burning, bursting into flames and it's falling on the morning fast.

492
00:37:08,320 --> 00:37:10,140
And all the folks between us, this is terrible.

493
00:37:10,260 --> 00:37:12,400
This is one of the worst catastrophes in the world.

494
00:37:12,401 --> 00:37:18,400
Oh, it's a place running cold, four or five hundred feet into the sky.

495
00:37:18,401 --> 00:37:21,400
And it's a terrific crash, ladies and gentlemen.

496
00:37:21,401 --> 00:37:25,400
The smoke is enslaved now, and the framers crashing to the ground,

497
00:37:25,401 --> 00:37:27,400
not quite to the mooring mass.

498
00:37:27,401 --> 00:37:29,400
Oh, the humanity and all the...

499
00:37:31,400 --> 00:37:34,400
I saw one man jump out of the nose,

500
00:37:34,401 --> 00:37:38,400
and when it was up there about 400 feet, and he got killed.

501
00:37:38,401 --> 00:37:46,800
We saw one man walk out and he was burnt from the top of his head to his heels.

502
00:37:46,801 --> 00:37:56,120
All he had on was his shoes.

503
00:37:56,121 --> 00:38:00,520
When we got to the passenger compartment, I mean there was people still in it.

504
00:38:00,521 --> 00:38:03,360
We told them to get out and we started looking around for some more.

505
00:38:03,361 --> 00:38:06,920
They wasn't even scratched.

506
00:38:06,921 --> 00:38:13,920
The Hindenburg, which took four years to build, was destroyed in approximately 37 seconds.

507
00:38:18,920 --> 00:38:25,920
What was but a moment ago, the queen of the air is now a shell of consuming flame, a tragic memory.

508
00:38:26,920 --> 00:38:33,920
On the night of May 6th, 1937, the greatest airship ever built lay in a smoldering twisted heap.

509
00:38:33,921 --> 00:38:35,920
I stood watch on it that night,

510
00:38:35,921 --> 00:38:37,920
trying to keep the looters out of it.

511
00:38:37,921 --> 00:38:38,920
But you couldn't do it.

512
00:38:38,921 --> 00:38:39,920
I mean, everybody's coming in,

513
00:38:39,921 --> 00:38:40,920
they want a little piece of this,

514
00:38:40,921 --> 00:38:42,920
a little piece of that.

515
00:38:42,921 --> 00:38:45,920
Miraculously, 62 people survived.

516
00:38:45,921 --> 00:38:47,920
The Americans and Germans held hearings

517
00:38:47,921 --> 00:38:52,920
to investigate the cause of the crash that killed 35.

518
00:38:52,921 --> 00:38:54,920
The Germans said it was sabotage, most of them,

519
00:38:54,921 --> 00:38:58,920
because they could not bring themselves

520
00:38:58,921 --> 00:39:00,920
to a frame of mind to admit

521
00:39:00,921 --> 00:39:02,920
that there could be any deficiency

522
00:39:02,921 --> 00:39:06,498
deficiency in the airship. Eckner was not the same way.

523
00:39:06,499 --> 00:39:08,840
I talked to him at some length about

524
00:39:08,841 --> 00:39:13,400
what did he, the greatest airship man of all time, think about what destroyed his airship.

525
00:39:14,120 --> 00:39:18,560
And he was convinced it made a sharp turn before it approached the mast.

526
00:39:18,561 --> 00:39:20,040
He thinks that there was

527
00:39:20,041 --> 00:39:23,480
a broken wire that slashed open a gas cell.

528
00:39:23,481 --> 00:39:27,720
That hydrogen escaped, caught and collected up inside

529
00:39:27,721 --> 00:39:34,720
the ship and started to leak out of it, and that the static electricity in the air was

530
00:39:34,780 --> 00:39:36,960
enough to set the thing off.

531
00:39:36,961 --> 00:39:43,960
I believe the Hindenburg crash as such marked the end of the airship as an effective passenger

532
00:39:45,080 --> 00:39:47,320
vehicle for intercontinental travel.

533
00:39:47,321 --> 00:39:50,680
You have to remember, airplanes had reached that magic

534
00:39:50,681 --> 00:39:57,680
point of technologically surpassing or at least achieving the same thing, even in a shorter

535
00:39:57,681 --> 00:40:00,180
of time to be truthful than the airships.

536
00:40:00,181 --> 00:40:03,920
So the days of the airship, the ridges were numbered.

537
00:40:03,921 --> 00:40:06,820
The Hindenburg is no more.

538
00:40:06,821 --> 00:40:09,220
But this catastrophe will not dim the vision,

539
00:40:09,221 --> 00:40:12,060
nor stop the work of the undaunted pioneers.

540
00:40:17,640 --> 00:40:19,300
The Germans completed another ship,

541
00:40:19,301 --> 00:40:22,380
virtually a sister ship of the Hindenburg,

542
00:40:22,381 --> 00:40:24,940
and it made something in the order of 40 flights

543
00:40:24,941 --> 00:40:29,940
in 1938 and 39, but it could never enter commercial service

544
00:40:31,180 --> 00:40:35,060
because the United States would not sell it helium.

545
00:40:35,061 --> 00:40:38,920
So, and nobody was going to ride a hydrogen filled

546
00:40:40,000 --> 00:40:41,360
passenger airship again.

547
00:40:49,360 --> 00:40:51,060
With the start of World War II,

548
00:40:51,061 --> 00:40:53,280
the Nazis deflated and dismantled

549
00:40:53,281 --> 00:40:55,520
the remaining rigid airships.

550
00:40:55,521 --> 00:40:57,680
The once peaceful giants of the sky

551
00:40:57,681 --> 00:41:01,080
were scrapped and melted down for fighter planes.

552
00:41:01,081 --> 00:41:04,420
The US Navy still was interested in airships,

553
00:41:04,421 --> 00:41:07,320
but knew that politically they could never

554
00:41:07,321 --> 00:41:09,660
build a really large one again.

555
00:41:09,661 --> 00:41:12,940
So they went instead to more blimps,

556
00:41:12,941 --> 00:41:15,100
small non-rigid airships.

557
00:41:16,100 --> 00:41:18,940
A fraction the size of the rigid airships.

558
00:41:19,940 --> 00:41:23,280
Their job was patrolling for U-boats

559
00:41:23,281 --> 00:41:27,060
And their squadrons were based all the way from Canada,

560
00:41:27,061 --> 00:41:30,220
down along the East Coast, the Gulf Coast,

561
00:41:30,221 --> 00:41:32,240
in the Caribbean, down to Brazil,

562
00:41:32,241 --> 00:41:35,520
and we flew them to Africa, to the Mediterranean.

563
00:41:35,521 --> 00:41:38,580
They escorted something like 70,000 ships,

564
00:41:39,640 --> 00:41:43,500
with only two lost to an enemy submarine attack.

565
00:41:44,420 --> 00:41:47,560
After the war, Hugo Eckner served as a consultant

566
00:41:47,561 --> 00:41:49,120
to the Goodyear Company.

567
00:41:49,121 --> 00:41:52,000
Prior to his death in 1954, he said,

568
00:41:52,001 --> 00:41:54,820
the Zeppelin has given way to the airplane.

569
00:41:54,821 --> 00:41:57,440
A good thing has been replaced by a better.

570
00:42:03,340 --> 00:42:06,520
Today, blimps are used primarily for advertising

571
00:42:06,521 --> 00:42:10,300
and can still draw the attention of everybody in sight.

572
00:42:10,301 --> 00:42:12,120
While their sophisticated mechanics

573
00:42:12,121 --> 00:42:15,980
make them much easier to steer than the Zeppelins of the past,

574
00:42:15,981 --> 00:42:19,360
blimps are nowhere near the size of the giant airships.

575
00:42:19,361 --> 00:42:26,440
An average blimp has about 100,000 cubic feet of gas, helium nowadays.

576
00:42:28,340 --> 00:42:31,340
The Hindenburg had 7.5 million cubic feet.

577
00:42:32,560 --> 00:42:36,340
Basically, a blimp today is about the length of the tail of the Hindenburg.

578
00:42:37,120 --> 00:42:42,340
A variety of blimps and tethered balloons are also used for communications platforms.

579
00:42:43,800 --> 00:42:47,680
Observation, radar, drug interdiction, and border patrols.

580
00:42:47,681 --> 00:42:53,580
They are all filled with non-flammable helium and seem to lack the romance, promise and

581
00:42:53,581 --> 00:42:58,860
adventure of the great rigid airships.

582
00:42:58,861 --> 00:43:03,060
More than 60 years after the crash of the Hindenburg, the Zeppelin company is building

583
00:43:03,061 --> 00:43:05,140
a revolutionary new airship.

584
00:43:05,141 --> 00:43:07,500
It's called the NT07.

585
00:43:07,501 --> 00:43:14,520
It is a rigid airship in design, although it has very little structure inside.

586
00:43:14,521 --> 00:43:19,840
The new Zeppelin will seat 12 and have a top speed of 90 miles an hour.

587
00:43:19,841 --> 00:43:25,420
Due to the airship's mechanical agility, its primary use will be for sightseeing.

588
00:43:25,421 --> 00:43:31,280
The advantage of the new Zeppelin is incorporated in its tail thruster unit, which will allow

589
00:43:31,281 --> 00:43:41,840
the pilot to control the airship left, right, nose up and nose down at zero airspeed.

590
00:43:41,841 --> 00:43:46,660
This ability will reduce the number of ground crew needed on takeoff and landing.

591
00:43:46,661 --> 00:43:52,880
The ship's technical manager is a distant relative of Count von Zeppelin himself.

592
00:43:52,881 --> 00:43:57,860
Five Zeppelins have already been sold for $6.8 million each.

593
00:43:57,861 --> 00:44:03,940
The Germans will use the NT-07 for tourism, advertising and scientific research.

594
00:44:03,941 --> 00:44:08,180
The Swiss plan to use the new airship for sightseeing.

595
00:44:08,181 --> 00:44:15,180
Another German company plans to build an enormous rigid airship to transport heavy cargo to faraway places.

596
00:44:15,181 --> 00:44:24,180
As far as I see from their plans, if they get away with this, their airship would be even much, much larger than the Hindenburg ever was.

597
00:44:24,181 --> 00:44:30,180
In fact, the planned cargo lifter is three and a half times the length of a 747

598
00:44:30,181 --> 00:44:37,820
and will have a payload of more than four times that of a jumbo jet or 175 tons.

599
00:44:38,880 --> 00:44:40,960
Estimated cost, $60 million.

600
00:44:43,040 --> 00:44:48,080
Located on a former Soviet military base, work has already begun on the hangar,

601
00:44:48,480 --> 00:44:50,300
which will house two cargo lifters.

602
00:44:51,000 --> 00:44:57,060
The huge airships will utilize solar energy and use one-tenth the fuel of a traditional 747.

603
00:44:57,061 --> 00:45:04,080
It seems within the foreseeable future, at least, that we may indeed see the rebirth of the rigid airship.

604
00:45:04,460 --> 00:45:08,200
And that prospect holds the same promise as the original Zeppelin's.

605
00:45:08,560 --> 00:45:12,400
If you are lucky enough to ever see one, you will never forget it.

